# irq_claim_test manifest — the device this test is allowed to own (#511).
#
# 🔴 IT USED TO NEED NONE, and its arm [7] is what that cost.  That arm asks
# every device on bus zero for a message-signalled interrupt, on the grounds
# that "which device has MSI-X is a property of the machine" -- true, and it
# required an authority no test should have: an MSI is programmed into the
# DEVICE's own table, so asking for one is a write to whoever owns it.
#
# ⚠️ WITHOUT THIS FILE THE ARM DEGRADED INSTEAD OF FAILING.  Every device
# refused, `accepted' reached zero, and the verdict for zero is "none, which
# is what a board with no MSI-X should say" -- a sentence that is false here
# and that the program has no way to tell from the true one.  A guard that
# stops measuring while still passing is the shape this tree keeps finding.
#
# 🔑 THE NETWORK CARD, and it is chosen for the same reason dma_reclaim_test
# chose the host bridge: nothing drives it.  There is no network driver in
# this tree -- that is the epic #479 -- so a test may hold it without taking
# anything away from anybody, and QEMU gives it an MSI-X table on Q35.
#
# ⚠️ The CLASS and not an address.  The card is 00:03.0 under one QEMU command
# line and elsewhere under another; the kernel re-reads the class out of the
# device's own configuration space and requires it to agree with what is
# declared here, so naming the wrong one fails loudly.

name irq_claim_test

# RESOURCE_PCI_DEVICE (5), class 0x020000 -- an Ethernet controller -- IRQ.
#
# 🔑 IRQ alone.  This test does not map the card's registers and does not
# program its DMA; it asks for an interrupt and gives it back.  A manifest
# that declared 0x7 out of symmetry with the drivers would be granting
# authority nobody asked for.
required 5 0x020000 0x4
